  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}(B)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $B$, that is, the rows of ${B}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${B}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${B}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${B}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${B}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(B)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $B,$ which is $\\text{Null}({B}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $B$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14093810/assets/18713ed3d103ca0a02d74cede1e6448012e0aa28a2df804d41cfb277589a0158.png)",
